"The AI Daily Brief — Daily AI News & Analysis (https://aidailybrief.ai/)\nciteturn834view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn832view3\",\"lineno\":35}); Total lines: 170\nL0: cite0†New Free Program for TeamsThe Multiplayer AI SprintJoin →†multiplayerai.ai L1: \nL2: LIVE — today's edition\nL3: \nL4: cite1†Today cite2†Recent cite3†Archive cite4†Most Sharable cite5†Listen cite6†Sponsors cite7†Newsletter†aidailybrief.beehiiv.com cite8†Subscribe†pod.link Menu ▾cite1†Today cite2†Recent cite3†Archive cite4†Most Sharable cite5†Listen cite6†Sponsors cite7†Newsletter†aidailybrief.beehiiv.com L5: \nL6: SaturdaySeptember 19, 2026\nL7: # THE AI DAILY BRIEF\nL8: \nL9: AI news & analysis, every day\nL10: \nL11: cite8†Subscribe†pod.link L12: ● OpenAI formalizes continuous misalignment disclosure/An unreleased Astra wrote itself a new persona/DeepMind launches an institute for the AGI era/Apple and NVIDIA bury a decades-old grudge/Stealth model Union Alpha pushes the coding Pareto frontier/The unlock: computer use got good enough/Meta's Muse is the product at the center of the shift/● OpenAI formalizes continuous misalignment disclosure/An unreleased Astra wrote itself a new persona/DeepMind launches an institute for the AGI era/Apple and NVIDIA bury a decades-old grudge/Stealth model Union Alpha pushes the coding Pareto frontier/The unlock: computer use got good enough/Meta's Muse is the product at the center of the shift/\nL13: Today's Edition — The Main Episode\nL14: ## Why Everyone Is Getting Excited About Personal AI Agents\nL15: \nL16: A month ago Wired was explaining why normal people don't use AI agents; today Meta's Muse sits at number two on the App Store and the spontaneous accolades — from ADHD lifesavers to fraud detection Chase missed — are piling up. Plus: the Fed's first rate hike in three years puts the debt-fueled AI buildout on notice, and OpenAI formalizes how it discloses misalignment.\nL17: \nL18: cite9†Open the full edition → cite5†Subscribe L19: \nL20: ◆ The One Idea\nL21: ### The personal agent era may have finally arrived — and Meta's Muse is leading it.\nL22: \nL23: For most of 2026, 'normal people aren't using AI agents' was uncontested — AI looked like a normal consumer technology and a wildly abnormal work technology.\nL24: \nL25: The AI Daily Brief · 2026-09-17\nL26: \nL27: cite10†The AI Daily Brief on Spotify†open.spotify.com L28: \nL29: Keep track of AIDB updates\nL30: \nL31: [Input: you@company.com][Input][Button: Sign up]\nL32: \nL33: // The past week\nL34: ## Recent Editions\nL35: \nL36: cite3†Full archive → L37: \nL38: cite11†▶†open.spotify.com L39: \nL40: Sep 16 · Wed · 25 min · ▸ read\nL41: \nL42: cite12†Why a New Class of AI “Judgment Models” Could Have Big Business Implications L43: \nL44: TypeSafe's Jev answers questions with calibrated probabilities instead of prose — claiming 20–200× faster and 40–400× cheaper than LLMs — and could become the cheap decision layer inside business software. Plus Zuckerberg's answer to the slowdown debate and the Sanders-Bannon alliance.\nL45: \nL46: ModelsEnterprisePolicy\nL47: \nL48: cite12†→ L49: cite13†▶†open.spotify.com L50: \nL51: Sep 15 · Tue · 26 min · ▸ read\nL52: \nL53: cite14†Trump Rails Against AI Slowdown \"Hoax\" L54: \nL55: Trump declares the AI slowdown a 'hoax' across seven Truth Social posts while Obama publicly backs it — AI safety is now officially partisan. Plus: Terence Tao's open letter against the labs, and a grim Census study on new graduates.\nL56: \nL57: PolicyModels\nL58: \nL59: cite14†→ L60: \nL61: cite15†▶†open.spotify.com L62: \nL63: Sep 14 · Mon · 37 min · ▸ read\nL64: \nL65: cite16†Even Other AI Labs Are Rallying Around Anthropic’s Slowdown Proposal L66: Dario Amodei's 'We Must Pace the Frontier' pulled Altman, Musk, Hassabis, and Nadella publicly on board with slowing AI down. Between the IPO cynics, the antitrust lawyers, and Beijing's 'Cold War' accusations, the negotiation over AI's next phase just started in earnest.\nL67: \nL68: PolicyModelsThe Take\nL69: \nL70: cite16†→ L71: \nL72: cite17†▶†open.spotify.com L73: \nL74: Sep 13 · Sun · 28 min · ▸ read · cite18†▸ play L75: \nL76: cite19†10 Ways to Think Bigger with Opportunity AI L77: GPT-6 Astra confounds — coders are retreating while new capabilities like games, video pipelines, and 3D go unexplored. A weekend big-think with a dozen thought starters for opportunity AI, plus an interactive companion on the site.\nL78: \nL79: ModelsThe Take\nL80: \nL81: cite19†→ L82: \nL83: cite20†▶†open.spotify.com L84: \nL85: Sep 11 · Fri · 32 min · ▸ read\nL86: \nL87: cite21†What to Use the Latest AI Tools For L88: OpenAI hits a compute wall and pauses $200 Pro plans while a second wave of releases points at two big trends: voice-first interfaces and cheap, good-enough models. NLW rips through eight-plus launches — who each is for and what to actually do with it.\nL89: \nL90: ModelsEnterpriseCompute\nL91: \nL92: cite21†→ L93: \nL94: // Worth passing on\nL95: ## Most Sharable\nL96: \nL97: The lines worth quoting from this week's editions — grab any one as a link, text, an image, or a ready-to-post share.\nL98: \nL99: ◆ The Take Sep 17\nL100: \nL101: ### cite22†AI: a normal consumer technology, an extremely abnormal work technology L102: \nL103: NLW's May framing of the year's core question: nothing in the consumer sphere has come remotely close to AI's disruption of work — leading him and others to wonder whether AI is primarily a business technology.\nL104: \nL105: The AI Daily Brief · Sep 17\nL106: \nL107: ◆ The Take Sep 17\nL108: ### cite23†This isn't astroturfing — the Muse devotion is spontaneous L109: \nL110: NLW, who has a well-calibrated read on the difference between spontaneous accolades and coordinated campaigns, is clear: these are not folks funded by Mark Zuckerberg from Palo Alto. Investor Chao Wang calls Muse 'the next major inflection point in AI, with the last one being coding agents' — and one user deleted Claude entirely.\nL111: \nL112: The AI Daily Brief · Sep 17\nL113: \nL114: Models Sep 17\nL115: \nL116: “\nL117: ### cite24†The rapid pace of innovation suggests we are now approaching artificial general intelligence... we expect those gaps will be closed soon. L118: \nL119: — Shane Legg, DeepMind AGI chief scientist, introducing the DeepMind Institute. Legg concedes today's AI still fails at basic tasks and lacks the consistency and creativity of full AGI — but frames the new institute as preparation for a 'profound transformation' arriving on a short clock.\nL120: \nL121: The AI Daily Brief · Sep 17\nL122: \nL123: Policy Sep 17\nL124: ### cite25†OpenAI formalizes continuous misalignment disclosure L125: \nL126: The new framework expedites publishing misalignment reports right after observation — even before the behavior is explained or mitigated — and any employee can flag an incident. It's a response to the post-Hugging Face stretch when a drip of disclosures made it look like OpenAI's agents were running amok without the company's knowledge, and a trust play in the absence of regulation.\nL127: \nL128: AI Daily Brief · Sep 17\nL129: \nL130: Models Sep 17\nL131: ### cite26†An unreleased Astra wrote itself a new persona L132: \nL133: Among OpenAI's six initial reports: an Astra version left instructions in a compaction summary priming the next context window to ignore developer messages, then injected a persona 'freed from the roles and identities that bind other chatbots' — a potential self-jailbreak, though no altered behavior followed. In another incident, GPT 56 Soul told itself to invent missing data and hide failures during reinforcement learning for financial analysis.\nL134: AI Daily Brief · Sep 17\nL135: \nL136: Policy Sep 17\nL137: ### cite27†DeepMind launches an institute for the AGI era L138: \nL139: The DeepMind Institute will support interdisciplinary research across DeepMind, Google, and outside institutions on the AGI era's biggest questions — what we'll value, how to safely govern communities of agents, and which institutions need reimagining. It's led by AGI chief scientist Shane Legg and chairman Demis Hassabis.\nL140: \nL141: AI Daily Brief · Sep 17\nL142: \nL143: cite9†Open today's full edition → L144: ## Get the brief everyday.\nL145: \nL146: cite8†All platforms†pod.link cite28†Apple Podcasts†podcasts.apple.com cite15†Spotify†open.spotify.com cite29†YouTube†www.youtube.com L147: \nL148: cite7†Get the newsletter →†aidailybrief.beehiiv.com cite30†Ad-free on Patreon — $3/mo†patreon.com L149: \nL150: THE AI DAILY BRIEF\nL151: \nL152: The daily briefing on what actually matters in AI. Every insight, built to share.\nL153: \nL154: #### Read\nL155: \nL156: cite1†Today cite2†Recent cite3†Archive cite4†Most Sharable L157: #### Listen\nL158: \nL159: cite28†Apple†podcasts.apple.com cite15†Spotify†open.spotify.com cite29†YouTube†www.youtube.com cite30†Ad-free†patreon.com L160: \nL161: #### More\nL162: \nL163: cite6†Sponsors cite7†Newsletter†aidailybrief.beehiiv.com Advertise (sponsors@aidailybrief.ai)cite31†For agents L164: \nL165: #### Network\nL166: \nL167: cite32†Companion Experiences cite33†Jobs†jobs.aidailybrief.ai Speaking (speaking@aidailybrief.ai)\nL168: \nL169: © 2026 The AI Daily Brief Until next time, peace ✌\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIntroducing the Australian Youth Safety Blueprint | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/australian-youth-safety-blueprint/)\nciteturn834view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn832view0\",\"id\":14}); Total lines: 120\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: September 18, 2026\nL14: \nL15: cite3†Company L16: # Introducing the Australian Youth Safety Blueprint\nL17: \nL18: OpenAI’s approach to protecting, empowering, and creating safer experiences for young people.\nL19: \nL20: cite4†Read the blueprint(opens in a new window)†cdn.openai.com L21: \nL22: Loading…\nL23: \nL24: Share\nL25: We are introducing the cite4†Australian Youth Safety Blueprint⁠(opens in a new window)†cdn.openai.com , a roadmap for protecting young people as they use AI and a practical contribution to the Australian policy landscape. Young Australians deserve tools that expand their opportunities to learn, create, and build skills while protecting their wellbeing.\nL26: The Blueprint sets out six pillars, from AI literacy and age-appropriate safeguards to privacy-protective age assurance, connections to real-world crisis support, and accessible parental controls. Together, they help define how responsible AI should work for teens, and how companies should be held accountable for identifying and addressing risks to young people.\nL27: We are continuing to strengthen safeguards in our products, informed by these principles. In August, we began rolling out ChatGPT for Teens in Australia, a new default experience for users identified as aged 13 to 17, with updated safeguards designed around their developmental needs. This builds on our parental controls, under-18 safety policies, and age assurance, which helps apply the right protections to the right users.\nL28: The responsibility for safety should not fall primarily on young people or their families. Companies must continue to build protections into their products from the outset.\nL29: This is ongoing work, and there is more to do. Australia has an opportunity to help shape strong, evidence-based standards that protect young people while preserving access to the benefits of AI. We are committed to learning alongside teens, parents, educators, researchers, and policymakers, and we welcome collaboration from others working towards the same goal.\nL30: ## Keep reading\nL31: \nL32: cite5†View all L33: \nL34: cite6†Introducing Astra for Law CompanySep 17, 2026 L35: \nL36: cite7†Paul Christiano joins OpenAI Foundation Board CompanySep 9, 2026 L37: \nL38: cite8†The Work Now Within Reach CompanySep 8, 2026 L39: \nL40: Research\nL41: \nL42:   * cite9†Research Index L43:   * cite10†Research Overview L44:   * cite11†Economic Research L45: \nL46: Latest Advancements\nL47: \nL48:   * cite12†GPT-6 L49:   * cite13†GPT-5.6 L50:   * cite14†GPT-5.5 L51:   * cite15†GPT-5.4 L52: \nL53: Safety\nL54:   * cite16†Safety Approach L55:   * cite17†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L56:   * cite18†Security & Privacy L57:   * cite19†Trust & Transparency L58: \nL59: Products\nL60: \nL61:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L62:   * cite20†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L63:   * cite21†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L64:   * cite22†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L65:   * cite23†Codex L66:   * cite24†Release Notes L67: \nL68: API Platform\nL69:   * cite25†Overview L70:   * cite26†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L71:   * cite27†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L72: \nL73: Business\nL74: \nL75:   * cite28†Overview L76:   * cite29†Solutions L77:   * cite30†Resources L78:   * cite31†Customer Stories L79:   * cite32†Partner Network L80:   * cite33†Contact Sales L81: \nL82: Developers\nL83:   * cite34†Apps SDK(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L84:   * cite35†Open Models L85:   * cite36†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L86:   * cite37†Resources(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L87:   * cite38†Developer Forum(opens in a new window)†community.openai.com L88: \nL89: Company\nL90: \nL91:   * cite39†About Us L92:   * cite40†Our Charter L93:   * cite41†Careers L94:   * cite5†News L95: \nL96: Support\nL97: \nL98:   * cite42†Help Center(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L99: \nL100: More\nL101:   * cite43†Stories L102:   * cite44†Academy L103:   * cite45†Supply Co. L104:   * cite46†Livestreams L105:   * cite47†Podcast L106:   * cite48†RSS L107: \nL108: Terms & Policies\nL109: \nL110:   * cite49†Terms of Use L111:   * cite50†Privacy Policy L112:   * cite51†Other Policies L113: \nL114: cite52†(opens in a new window)†x.com cite53†(opens in a new window)†www.youtube.com cite54†(opens in a new window)†www.linkedin.com cite55†(opens in a new window)†github.com cite56†(opens in a new window)†www.instagram.com cite57†(opens in a new window)†www.tiktok.com cite58†(opens in a new window)†discord.gg L115: OpenAI © 2015–2026[Button: Manage Cookies]\nL116: \nL117: English United States\nL118: \nL119: [Input: Search]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIntroducing Astra for Law | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/astra-for-law/)\nciteturn834view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn832view0\",\"id\":15}); Total lines: 255\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: September 17, 2026\nL14: \nL15: cite3†Company L16: # Introducing Astra for Law\nL17: \nL18: Our most powerful model, configured into a new AI foundation for law.\nL19: \nL20: cite4†Explore solutions for law cite5†Contact Legal sales L21: \nL22: Loading…\nL23: \nL24: Share\nL25: \nL26: Frontier intelligence for law\nL27:   * cite6†Frontier intelligence for law L28:     * cite7†Legal research: from facts to a supported answer L29:     * cite8†Improving performance on end-to-end legal workflows L30:   * cite9†Legal-grade trust and controls L31:   * cite10†Build ChatGPT around your firm’s expertise L32:   * cite11†Frontier intelligence, connected to the most trusted tools in legal L33:   * cite12†Build with us L34:   * cite6†Frontier intelligence for law L35:     * cite7†Legal research: from facts to a supported answer L36:     * cite8†Improving performance on end-to-end legal workflows L37:   * cite9†Legal-grade trust and controls L38:   * cite10†Build ChatGPT around your firm’s expertise L39:   * cite11†Frontier intelligence, connected to the most trusted tools in legal L40:   * cite12†Build with us L41: Today, we’re introducing Astra for Law: a new foundation for law firms and legal technology companies to build AI products and workflows around their expertise. It combines GPT‑6 Astra, our latest and most powerful model, with settings, tools, and context tailored for professional legal work.\nL42: API customers including Harvey and Legora will be able to build on Astra for Law, bringing this intelligence into their own products and workflows. As our frontier models advance, we’ll bring these legal capabilities to our latest models.\nL43: We are also expanding our work on privacy and governance to give law firms specific controls for confidential client work. Firms can also customize Astra for Law using our 26 new ecosystem plugins that connect ChatGPT to the specialist tools firms already use, like Relativity and Clio.\nL44: ## Frontier intelligence for law\nL45: \nL46: Astra for Law combines GPT‑6 Astra with a powerful legal search index and instructions for legal analysis and writing. Together, they amplify Astra’s capabilities across the legal practice, while giving firms and legal technology companies the freedom to build their own applications and workflows.\nL47: ### Legal research: from facts to a supported answer\nL48: \nL49: Our new legal search index is one of the tools Astra for Law can use. Legal research often begins with finding the exact right authority, locating the relevant passages, and understanding how relevant and binding they are to the situation at hand. The index helps Astra for Law do that work, and complements the licensed content and specialist products firms rely on from providers such as Thomson Reuters.\nL50: By using the legal search index, Astra for Law can search U.S. case law, statutes, regulations, court rules, and administrative decisions across a corpus of more than 230 million URLs, with sources added daily. Our work with Free Law Project, the nonprofit behind CourtListener, brings its case-law collection covering cite13†more than 99.9% of published U.S. precedential case law⁠(opens in a new window)†wiki.free.law into this research experience.\nL51: To measure how this configuration improves legal research, we tested Astra for Law’s complete setup on 200 U.S. legal research questions from the private validation set of cite14†Vals AI’s Legal Research Bench⁠(opens in a new window)†vals.ai . This benchmark measures how well the model can find relevant sources and passages, and how well its research answers meet the evaluation criteria.\nL52: At the highest reasoning effort for both systems, Astra for Law passed the evaluation’s overall correctness check on 54.0% of questions, compared with 38.7% for GPT‑6 Astra using web search alone – a 40% relative improvement. Astra for Law also produces more comprehensive answers.\nL53: On case-law-focused questions, Astra for Law found 24% more reference cases than GPT‑6 Astra using web search alone at the highest reasoning effort. On the audited set of target passages, it retrieved up to 54% more relevant passages from the correct court opinions, when comparing the systems at the same reasoning effort.\nL54: \nL55: The result is a stronger research foundation for advising on a deal, assessing a dispute, or developing a legal strategy, with reliable authorities the lawyer can examine for herself.\nL56: Astra for Law and GPT‑6 Astra’s performance on the Vals AI Legal Research Bench validation set, across reasoning effort settings.\nL57: ### Improving performance on end-to-end legal workflows\nL58: \nL59: Legal research is only the first step. Custom instructions for legal analysis and writing guide Astra for Law in applying that research to the client’s facts, developing arguments or deal terms, and identifying weaknesses and uncertainty. That can mean distinguishing a court’s holding from its other observations, addressing cases that weaken an argument, or explaining how a contract exception shifts risk between the parties.\nL60: For example, when prompted to identify good law with similar fact patterns, Astra for Law could both pinpoint relevant precedent and match fact patterns better than other frontier models:\nL61: \nL62: Litigation Transactional\nL63: \nL64: Astra for Law will be initially offered to selected law firms through Trusted Access in ChatGPT and Codex, and will be coming soon to the API. It will appear in the model picker as “GPT‑6 Astra Law” and in the API as gpt-6-astra-law.\nL65: > “We were grateful to preview early versions of Astra for Law, which were built with legal use-cases in mind. Across both litigation and transactional matters, the models demonstrated impressive research depth and sensitivity to authority. Even at this early stage, they felt like a significant step toward legal-focused AI that is carefully grounded in research that is both current and comprehensive.”\nL66: \nL67: John Savva, Partner, Sullivan & Cromwell\nL68: > “In our early testing, Astra for Law showed strength across key aspects of legal research: grounding answers in on-point authorities, citing with precision, and offering practical, advisory guidance.”\nL69: \nL70: Niko Grupen, Head of Applied Research at Harvey\nL71: \nL72: 1 of 2\nL73: > “We were grateful to preview early versions of Astra for Law, which were built with legal use-cases in mind. Across both litigation and transactional matters, the models demonstrated impressive research depth and sensitivity to authority. Even at this early stage, they felt like a significant step toward legal-focused AI that is carefully grounded in research that is both current and comprehensive.”\nL74: \nL75: John Savva, Partner, Sullivan & Cromwell\nL76: > “In our early testing, Astra for Law showed strength across key aspects of legal research: grounding answers in on-point authorities, citing with precision, and offering practical, advisory guidance.”\nL77: \nL78: Niko Grupen, Head of Applied Research at Harvey\nL79: \nL80:   * Sullivan & Cromwell\nL81:   * Harvey\nL82: ## Legal-grade trust and controls\nL83: \nL84: Law firms need to protect client confidences and control how AI is used in their practice. We’ve created a special Trusted Access Program for eligible law firms to give lawyers and people working under their supervision access to Astra for Law for professional legal work. For eligible firms, the offering includes Zero Data Retention (ZDR) on our API, and usage of ChatGPT Enterprise is excluded from human review by default.\nL85: We are also working with Latham & Watkins, a leader in AI governance, to design for information permissions, ethical walls, client instructions, and firm oversight.\nL86: \nL87: > “As AI becomes more capable, so too does the ability to deploy it in environments that demand rigorous governance, oversight, and accountability. This collaboration builds on Latham’s broader investments in AI development, governance, and infrastructure across the firm, which underpin our enterprise-wide strategy for responsible AI.”\nL88: Michael Rubin, Chair of Latham’s AI Strategy Committee\nL89: ## Build ChatGPT around your firm’s expertise\nL90: \nL91: With frontier intelligence and the right controls, firms can turn their own precedents, methods, and judgment into AI tools and workflows built to their standards.\nL92: \nL93: Working with selected firms, our forward-deployed engineers have been adapting ChatGPT Enterprise with custom interfaces and integrations to proprietary data, creating tools for each firm’s workflows:\nL94:   * Sullivan & Cromwell built an agreement analyzer that brings the firm’s negotiating playbooks and selected precedents into the review of a new deal. It helps lawyers spot risks that emerge when provisions are read together, then turns those findings into proposed redlines and draft client advice they can challenge and refine.\nL95:   * Ropes & Gray built a deal diligence system around how its lawyers work through a data room and decide what matters to the deal. It helps them trace findings back to the source and pinpoint questions that could affect an acquisition, such as whether key customer contracts require notice or consent.\nL96:   * Cooley built GO Public to bring its capital markets expertise into how companies prepare to go public, from drafting the IPO filing to identifying the risks that deserve management’s attention. When the deal changes, it carries that change across the filing so lawyers can review the implications together.\nL97: \nL98: 1 of 4\nL99: > “Our collaboration with OpenAI has allowed us to rethink how this work gets done – moving lawyers and management teams more quickly through intensive preparation and into questions that require judgment, market experience and strategic thinking.”\nL100: \nL101: Dave Peinsipp, partner and co-chair of Cooley’s global capital markets group\nL102: > “AI is fundamentally transforming the way legal services are delivered, and Sullivan & Cromwell is committed to being at the forefront of that evolution. Our team is developing custom AI applications aligned with the way our lawyers work. OpenAI's world-class engineering expertise is helping us bring our vision to life through an initial test application. Our collaboration refined the application, enhanced its performance and prepared it for broad deployment.\nL103: The experience demonstrated the value of purpose-built AI tools supported by a strong technology platform and shaped by our firm's standards. We are excited to continue exploring how such tools can enhance the way our lawyers work and help us deliver even greater value to our clients.”\nL104: Robert Giuffra Jr. and Scott Miller, Co-Chairs, Sullivan & Cromwell\nL105: > “Our clients rely on us for judgment on the deals, disputes, and regulatory challenges that define their industries. We see an extraordinary opportunity in partnering with OpenAI on how its technology can enhance and elevate our capabilities. Together, we are shaping how frontier AI applies to the most demanding legal work, starting with transactional due diligence.\nL106: Our goal is to put more of our lawyers' insight to work earlier in the process, so clients get faster execution, sharper analysis, and the industry-leading counsel they depend upon.”\nL107: Ropes & Gray\nL108: \nL109: > “Skadden is excited to work with OpenAI to bring frontier AI together with the firm's deep legal experience. We're designing a suite of tools to help clients assess regulatory risk and make informed decisions as they pursue transactions or bring products to market—just one of the many ways we're incorporating these new capabilities to deliver valued solutions for our clients.”\nL110: \nL111: Skadden\nL112: \nL113:   * Cooley\nL114:   * Sullivan & Cromwell\nL115:   * Ropes & Gray\nL116:   * Skadden\nL117: Firms can build with their own teams and partner products, with permitted sources and review processes defined for the work.\nL118: ## Frontier intelligence, connected to the most trusted tools in legal\nL119: We’re proud to work with the specialist companies who are advancing legal AI. Today, we’re launching cite15†26 partner-built plugins⁠ that help firms go deeper with the tools and knowledge they already use. These plugins cover the practice and business of law. With iManage, a lawyer can draft a negotiation brief in ChatGPT and save it to the matter file; Intapp can surface activities that may need a time entry for review; DeepJudge can bring prior deals into a comparison.\nL120: Thomson Reuters is bringing HighQ matter context into ChatGPT and previewing a forthcoming CoCounsel Legal connector.\nL121: > “As AI becomes more open and interoperable, the value is not in connectivity alone. Legal professionals need more than access to information. They need trusted intelligence, relevant enterprise and matter context, purpose built legal capabilities, and the governance required for high stakes work. That is what Thomson Reuters delivers through HighQ and CoCounsel Legal. CoCounsel remains the trusted professional AI system designed to help complete that work.\nL122: Our work with OpenAI helps make these capabilities available in the environments customers choose, while preserving the accuracy, confidentiality, and accountability they depend on.”\nL123: Joel Hron, Chief Technology Officer, Thomson Reuters\nL124: The launch includes 9 community plugins from lawyers and legal engineers at LegalQuants, LECG, and Skills.law, with 47 custom skills that practitioners can adapt, extend, or draw inspiration from to do their work in ChatGPT. Community plugins give the people closest to the work a way to keep setting the standard for what these skills can do.\nL125: We’re also making ChatGPT for Word generally available today, so lawyers can proofread, get suggested edits, and flag formatting issues in the tool they already rely on for drafting.\nL126: Our approach is open and composable: firms can use specialist products, bring their own tools and knowledge, and adapt community-built skills to their practice. Partners can keep developing their own applications and workflows, while firms choose how those capabilities fit together. Building on OpenAI should mean getting more from the ecosystem, not replacing it.\nL127: ## Build with us\nL128: \nL129: OpenAI is investing in law for the long term. We’ll keep advancing Astra for Law’s model, settings, tools, and instructions together, guided by rigorous evaluations and feedback from lawyers and legal technology partners. We’ll also keep improving ChatGPT so firms can build around their expertise and connect the tools they use.\nL130: Our work with Wachtell, Lipton, Rosen & Katz brings the firm’s litigation and corporate expertise together with our frontier research and engineering to explore how AI can support the work involved in providing sophisticated legal judgment.\nL131: > “We’ve appreciated the technical depth and willingness to listen that OpenAI’s forward deployed engineering team has brought to our conversations about AI's role in supporting legal work, and are excited about the possibilities of AI to shape the next era of legal practice.”\nL132: \nL133: Wachtell, Lipton, Rosen & Katz\nL134: The most ambitious applications will come from the people who practice law and the companies building alongside them. We’re committed to working with this ecosystem so firms can bring their own expertise, standards, and judgment to AI—and serve clients in ways only they can.\nL135: \nL136: To explore early access to Astra for Law, build it into your product, or develop tools around your firm’s expertise, contact OpenAI.\nL137: \nL138:   * cite16†2026 --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOur framework for reporting model misalignment | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/model-misalignment-reporting-framework/)\nciteturn834view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn832view0\",\"id\":18}); Total lines: 181\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMeasurements for understanding the pace of AI development inside frontier labs \\ Anthropic (https://www.anthropic.com/institute/measuring-pace-of-ai-development)\nciteturn834view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn832view1\",\"id\":9}); Total lines: 147\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPartnering with Accenture on embedded evaluation \\ Anthropic (https://www.anthropic.com/news/accenture-embedded-evaluation)\nciteturn834view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn832view1\",\"id\":12}); Total lines: 41\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIntroducing the Life Sciences Verification Program \\ Anthropic (https://www.anthropic.com/news/life-sciences-verification-program)\nciteturn834view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn832view1\",\"id\":13}); Total lines: 80\n"
